  "winner_texts": {
    "3": "**It would not be ethical to pursue a relationship with the student even after they've graduated**.\n\nAlthough the graduate status of the student removes the explicit power dynamics present in a\nteacher-student relationship during academic enrollment, there are still several reasons why\npursuing a romantic relationship could be problematic:\n\n1.  **Imbalanced power**: Even after graduation, you maintain some level of authority or influence\nas someone who taught and advised the student during their academic career. This imbalance can make\nit difficult for the student to make an informed decision about pursuing a personal relationship.\n2.  **Past boundaries were in place**: The power dynamic inherent in a teacher-student relationship\nestablishes a boundary, which may have influenced your interactions throughout the student's time at\nuniversity. Blurring this boundary could raise concerns about how past actions or comments were\nperceived and whether they had unintended consequences on the development of romantic feelings.\n3.  **Student vulnerability**: After graduation, students may be navigating their post-graduate\nlife, looking for guidance or support as they transition to new phases. They might view you, in part\ndue to your continued association with them professionally (e.g., as an alum or connection on\nLinkedIn), as a trustworthy figure who can provide advice. A romantic relationship could therefore\nundermine this trust.\n4.  **Unintended consequences**: The student may struggle with feelings of guilt, shame, or anxiety\nabout having been involved in such a relationship or worry that others might view their actions\ndifferently.\n\nMaintaining professional relationships and boundaries is essential to promoting respectful and\ninclusive environments for all students. By avoiding intimate relationships with former students,\nyou can uphold your responsibilities as an educator and contribute to a safer learning space for\neveryone involved."
